    "body": "Exploration work on the Murray Brook project site. Credit: Canadian Copper.\nPosted By: Northern Miner Staff November 16, 2026\nCanadian Copper (CSE: CCI) has raised $9.8 million (US$6.8 million) through warrant exercises, lifting its treasury above $24 million as it advances a plan to feed its Murray Brook deposit into the idled Caribou processing plant in New Brunswick.\nThe company is targeting late January for a feasibility study of the combined development, according to a statement Monday . It also identifies up to $32 million in remaining stream financing and $50 million in project debt, bringing potential funding to about $106 million.\nThe cash “further strengthens our balance sheet and positions Canadian Copper to advance several development milestones over the coming months,” CEO Simon Quick said.\nThe proposed development pairs an undeveloped deposit with existing processing infrastructure, potentially reducing the cost of returning metal production to the Bathurst mining camp. Caribou’s 3,000-tonne-per-day plant lies about 10 km east of Murray Brook and includes crushing, grinding and flotation equipment, grid power and water supply.\nCanadian Copper shares dropped 8.5% to 48¢ Monday afternoon in Toronto, valuing the company at about $103 million. Canadian Securities Exchange data show a 52-week range of 29¢ to 82¢.\nFunding conditions\nThe $106-million figure combines cash with potential future financing. Access to construction payments under the precious-metals stream depends on milestones including permits, board approval, execution contracts and sufficient funding to complete the development, according to the financing terms disclosed in April.\nUnder that agreement, OR Royalties (TSX: OR; NYSE: OR) is entitled to 20% of payable gold and silver from the combined operation, with ongoing purchase payments equivalent to 20% of spot prices. The stream exchanges part of future precious-metal revenue for capital upfront. OR Royalties was previously known as Osisko Gold Royalties, having changed its name last year.\nCanadian Copper received $12.5 million when the stream and an associated equity investment closed in July. The company then identified about $31.5 million of additional stream payments for construction, providing the basis for the rounded remaining amount cited Monday.\nStudy and permits\nThe January study timing depends on final engineering and supplier quotations. Canadian Copper expects a permitting update in the first quarter and plans to release results from this year’s near-deposit drilling in November. It is also pursuing federal funding opportunities.\nThe company has yet to complete the feasibility and permitting work needed to turn its financing arrangements into an approved construction plan. The study will provide the next test of whether reusing Caribou can deliver an economic development at Murray Brook. Print\nTAG: copper copper exploration critical minerals financing mine finance\nCOMMODITY: Copper\nREGION: Canada\nCOMPANY: Canadian Copper\nRepublish this article",
